            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 85% efficient oil boiler. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane boiler with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE.



Your hot water is also heated by the boiler. Installing a 95% condensing propane furnace will also save you money on your hot water. 



While I was on site, I noticed a significant amount of soot around the chimney of your boiler. I recommend regular servicing of the boiler and dedicated CO detectors in your basement as this could indicate an issue. 



Savings: By replacing your current oil boiler with a 95% AFUE condensing propane boiler, you could potentially save $550 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 20.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). While updating your boiler, you should also install ECM circulation pumps and qualify for up to $175 in rebates per pump (up to 2 per fiscal year) from EEIP. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

In general, your home is already quite energy efficient. However, I did make note of some small things that you can do.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM Pumps

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency. These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



I recommend that you replace your hydronic circulation pumps with ECM circulation pumps. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. In addition, LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test = Fail

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 41 Pascals. As this number is much higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment may cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your boiler is currently directly vented to the outside of your house, and you do not have any wood stoves. .



During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:

        - three bathroom fans

        - a clothes dryer

        - a range hood



You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat, which you have already installed.



Your home also has a heat recovery ventilator (HRV). I recommend that you open this up and take a look at the condition of the vents and the filters. HRV filters need to be cleaned regularly to function effectively. You can vacuum dust out with a regular household vacuum and the filters can be washed with water and mild dish detergent.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Air Sealing and Back Door

Your home is already quite air tight. However, I recommend that you check the weather stripping around your back door as there was a significant amount of air leakage through it, to the point where it was frozen shut during my assessment. I recommend replacing this weather stripping once the weather warms up. In the meantime, using a draft blocker at the bottom of the door will keep drafts out of your kitchen. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Windows

All of your windows are already triple pane, with one low-emissivity coating, and are hinged windows. This is in line with our current recommendations, and as your windows are in good physical condition I do not recommend replacing them at this time. However, I did notice a lot of condensation and ice build up on these windows, particularly at windows where you have the curtains drawn. Condensation can be controlled by increasing the amount of air circulating over the window panes, so I recommend that you open the curtains during the day to allow air to move over the windows. Increasing the amount of time you run the HRV will also allow more air circulation. 



I did see some mold on some of the window frames, particularly where the ice build up is quite significant. Allow these windows to dry and wipe the mold up with household cleaner. Allowing air circulation will also prevent mold in the future, as it requires moisture. 



Should you need to replace any windows for any reason such as physical damage, we currently recommend recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.25 BTU per hour.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs ????????? Yellowknife

The following December 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:



Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil - $0.91 per liter 

Propane - $0.608 per liter delivered

Wood - $420 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.51 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



